  "steps": [
    {
      "type": "explain",
      "title": "Roman numeral",
      "body": "Musicians number the chords of a key with a **Roman numeral** for each scale degree: I II III IV V VI VII. Upper case means a major chord, lower case means minor, and ° means diminished. In C major the seven chords are I ii iii IV V vi vii°.\n\n**Why it matters:** Numerals work in every key. \"I–IV–V\" is the same idea in C, in G or in E♭, so one pattern covers every song that uses it.",
      "exampleMidi": [
        60,
        62,
        64,
        65,
        67,
        69,
        71
      ],
      "labels": {
        "60": "I: C",
        "62": "ii: Dm",
        "64": "iii: Em",
        "65": "IV: F",
        "67": "V: G",
        "69": "vi: Am",
        "71": "vii°: B°"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "show",
      "title": "Upper and lower case",
      "body": "I, IV and V are major (upper case). ii, iii and vi are minor (lower case).",
